WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.590
[Dr. Ochsner:] Carcinoma of the lung is a serious disease,

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:41.930
We are all very much concerned about it,

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:45.660
not only because it is the most frequent of all cancers,

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.860
but also because the prognosis is so bad.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:50.430
The reason the prognosis is bad,

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:54.650
is that carcinoma of the lung is early [?] invasive.

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:56.200
We found that of all the patients

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:59.260
going through our pulmonary function laboratory,

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:02.280
in whom we find a cancer of the lung,

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.720
that 40 percent of them have tumor cells

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:07.150
in their peripheral arterial blood.

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:11.620
In those in which the lesion is in the periphery it's 70 percent,

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:13.160
whereas in those in which the lesion

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:18.400
is in the hilar portion of the lung, it's 30 percent.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.630
The other tragic thing about cancer of the lung

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:23.850
is that it is largely preventable.

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:26.500
With the exception of adenocarcinoma,

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:27.870
which is a rare disease,

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:31.480
and is proportionately becoming rarer all the time,

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.290
and which has no relationship to the etiologic factor

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:36.230
I'm going to discuss in a moment,

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:39.910
all other cancers of the lung are caused by cigarette smoking.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:43.490
So this is a disease which has become the most frequent of all cancers,

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:44.890
which is preventable,

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:49.040
and which is one of our great public health hazards today.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:50.230
As I mentioned previously,

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:52.980
the results from the treatment of cancer of the lung are not good.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.770
As shown in this graph of all the patients that we've seen,

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:58.450
now representing over 3500,

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:01.290
only 50 percent are operable.

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:03.260
In other words 50 percent of them,

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:07.860
the lesion is already extended beyond the lung at the time we see them.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:11.110
Only 30 percent of the entire group are resectable,

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:14.220
and only six percent of the entire group are

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:19.950
alive and apparently free from disease at the end of five years.

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:22.090
This is a man 52 years of age

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:25.680
who began with some mild discomfort in his left chest.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:26.970
An x-ray was taken.

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:28.730
It showed a shadow in his lung.

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:32.410
This increased rather rapidly within a very short period of time.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:36.950
As one can see here in the left, uhh, chest is a shadow,

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:38.840
apparently attached to the chest wall.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:41.480
From this we can't tell whether it's in the lung,

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:44.890
or whether it originated from the parietes.

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:47.180
In the [?] roentgenogram or the black rather,

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:50.000
one can see that this is in the parenchyma.

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.010
This man was a very heavy smoker,

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:54.040
and because of his heavy smoking,

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:55.770
we feel that quite definitely

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:58.400
this is a bronchogenic carcinoma.

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:02.900
We feel that a history of smoking is the most positive method

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:06.410
of making a diagnosis of cancer of the lung.

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:10.770
Cytology here showed tumor cells in the sputum.

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:14.730
Incision is made over the left fifth rib.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:18.480
It begins anteriorly, curves around the angle of the scapula,

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:21.840
and then extends up superiorly.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:25.000
The patient is in the right lateral decubitus,

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:32.840
so that the left chest is up.

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:35.420
One can either do the operation

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:37.430
through an intercostal incision

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:39.500
or one can resect the rib.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:41.790
Now I'm going to resect the rib in this case,

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:44.230
because of the lateral area,

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:45.400
the position of it.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:47.680
Now I'm counting the ribs,

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:48.900
because before the incision

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:52.420
it's impossible to tell just which is the fifth rib.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.520
But beginning above I am counting them,

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:57.900
and now here is the fifth rib, and I am going to resect this.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:01.190
Because of the lateral position of the tumor,

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:04.950
I think that it is probably adhered to the parietes,

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:08.950
and I think there's less danger of my getting into tumor

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:10.600
if I resect the rib

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:15.670
than if I make an intercostal incision.

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:17.250
Also I think they'll have less pain

00:04:17.270 --> 00:04:27.820
if their rib is resected rather than an intercostal incision.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:30.340
Periosteum is pulled away from the rib.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:33.290
The resection is done sub-posteriorly

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:46.370
and this helps to stabilize the chest later.

00:04:46.390 --> 00:04:48.690
Beginning posteriorly on the upper side

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:51.420
and anteriorly on the lower side,

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:53.540
we separate the periosteum.

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:56.010
The periosteum is further separated from the rib

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:59.940
with this instrument we call a joker.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:03.640
And then using the Doyen Periosteal Elevator,

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:11.410
the periosteum is separated from the rib.

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:24.580
Costotome is used to divide the rib both anteriorly and posteriorly.

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:29.540
Now I'm incising through the bed of the rib,

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:34.420
and as I feared the lung is adhered in this area.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:38.260
I'm going to try and get an area where it is free,

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:41.160
because I want to be able to tell more about

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:45.350
just what this attachment is due to.

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:49.600
Now I'm in the free pleural cavity posteriorly,

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:54.630
and as I feared the lung is adhered to the parietes,

00:05:54.650 --> 00:05:58.490
and because this adherence may be neoplastic,

00:05:58.510 --> 00:06:04.340
I'm going to do an extra-pleural dissection of the lung.

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:06.610
Now using this periosteal elevator,

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:11.580
I'm separating the pleura from the uhh, chest wall,

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:18.040
and in this way, if the pleura is involved with neoplastic lesion,

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:23.620
there is not much likelihood of implantation of tumor.

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:24.990
Now I've separated it;

00:06:25.010 --> 00:06:29.920
one can see how the parietal pleura is adhered to the lung.

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:41.470
Now dividing the pleura away from the chest wall above,

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:43.370
now we're dividing it inferiorly;

00:06:43.390 --> 00:06:50.770
this is a part that we separated away from the parietes.

00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:52.460
One can see the tumor...

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:57.570
this is the attachment of the pleura to the lung and tumor.

00:06:57.590 --> 00:07:00.710
Of course now we can't tell whether it's neoplastic or not.

00:07:00.730 --> 00:07:03.590
I'm inclined to believe that it's only inflammatory.

00:07:03.610 --> 00:07:08.930
The ribs are separated by means of the finochietto retractor.

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:11.870
One can see this is a mediastinum.

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:17.680
There is a phrenic nerve lying about the mid-portion of the frame.

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:22.070
We're now freeing the mediastinum partly by blood dissection,

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:24.800
partly by sharp dissection.

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:31.160
We'll get up and isolate the pulmonary artery.

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:35.690
There are a number of small branches here, the pulmonary plexus,

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:40.570
which are nerve fibers but there are small vessels along with them,

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:45.540
and we are now getting a SEMB ligature carrier

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:48.200
around the pulmonary artery.

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:50.500
This is the main branch of the pulmonary artery.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:53.520
The reason we do this is because although we don't know yet

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:57.640
whether we are going to do pneumonectomy or lobectomy,

00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:01.930
is that by isolating the pulmonary artery and getting a ligature on it,

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:05.860
we can control any bleeding if it might occur

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:10.320
in severing the branches of the pulmonary artery.

00:08:10.340 --> 00:08:15.240
This is another small branch, uh, vessel in the mediastinum,

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:19.840
which we are ligating to prevent troublesome bleeding.

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:22.700
This is another branch of the pulmonary plexus [?]

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:33.210
dividing, this will be ligated also.

00:08:33.230 --> 00:08:36.830
Freeing the mediastinum...so far I haven't encountered

00:08:36.850 --> 00:08:42.340
any large nodes which appear to be involved.

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:47.140
The uh, node under the, the nodes under the arch of aorta

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:50.660
apparently are uninvolved.

00:08:50.680 --> 00:09:05.620
This is a pulmonary artery again which I'm putting the ligature around.

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:07.110
This is simply a precautionary measure,

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:09.380
because as I said, I don't know whether we are going to do a

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:12.620
lobectomy or a pneumonectomy.

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:15.340
I'm now separating the individual branches

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:19.880
of the pulmonary artery because uhh, I am going to

00:09:19.900 --> 00:09:23.380
do a lobectomy if it seems feasible and I can remove it.

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:26.840
The reason for this is, as I mentioned earlier,

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:31.550
the curability incidence in carcinoma of the lung is low,

00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:35.910
and we've learned by sad experience that the best way to,

00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:38.060
the best way to treat cancer of the lung

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:50.860
is to do as little resection as one can and remove the tumor grossly.

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:55.720
Dividing the superior branch of the pulmonary artery...

00:09:55.740 --> 00:10:02.670
it has been ligated, I'll transfix this.

00:10:02.690 --> 00:10:05.900
Because it's important to transfix these vessels

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:09.270
since they are large vessels in diameter.

00:10:09.290 --> 00:10:10.650
As a matter of fact, their diameter

00:10:10.670 --> 00:10:13.990
is actually larger than their length.

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:17.720
And it's almost like uh, ligating a funnel.

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:22.550
So that unless one transfixes it one will uh, there's likely to be bleeding.

00:10:22.570 --> 00:10:23.610
Now I'm getting some bleeding

00:10:23.630 --> 00:10:27.070
from the inferior branch of the pulmonary artery.

00:10:27.090 --> 00:10:30.320
At this stage I didn't know what was causing this,

00:10:30.340 --> 00:10:32.050
but we subsequently found out that this was

00:10:32.070 --> 00:10:35.300
due to involvement of the artery by the tumor.

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:38.460
Because of this, we decided to do a pneumonectomy,

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:41.540
because we felt that we would not cure this man

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:44.380
if we didn't do a pneumonectomy.

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:47.620
So now the pulmonary artery which I previously,

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:51.030
around which I previously put a ligature,

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:54.220
is now being ligated.

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:56.840
We put one on medially and distally,

00:10:56.860 --> 00:11:01.110
and then we put two transfixion sutures medially.

00:11:01.130 --> 00:11:02.640
These are placed in such a way that

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:05.100
four quadrants of the vessel are caught.

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:08.530
One goes superiorly inferiorly

00:11:08.550 --> 00:11:11.280
and the other goes anteriorly posteriorly.

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:14.280
So that we have four quadrants of the vessel caught.

00:11:14.300 --> 00:11:16.240
As I previously mentioned,

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:21.460
this is necessary because of the large diameter of the vessel,

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:24.210
and if one doesn't catch the four quadrants,

00:11:24.230 --> 00:11:28.510
there is likelihood of slippage of the vessel,

00:11:28.530 --> 00:11:30.910
the diameter of the vessel being actually larger

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:38.090
than the length of the vessel.

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:39.810
Using this technique, however,

00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:43.810
there is little or no danger of hemorrhage,

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:48.960
and this I found through the years to be a very valuable method

00:11:48.980 --> 00:11:53.600
of handling the pulmonary vessels.

00:11:53.620 --> 00:11:59.580
Both the artery and the two pulmonary veins.

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:05.290
The first one, first transfixion ligature went superiorly and inferiorly,

00:12:05.310 --> 00:12:08.950
and this one, the second one, goes anteriorly posteriorly,

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:19.250
so that the four quadrants are caught.

00:12:19.270 --> 00:12:23.640
After tying it anteriorly, the suture is pulled around

00:12:23.660 --> 00:12:36.240
and tied posteriorly so as to get the entire segment.

00:12:36.260 --> 00:12:43.570
We now divide the vessel leaving a relatively long segment.

00:12:43.590 --> 00:12:48.360
Now we're going to free the inferior pulmonary ligament

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:50.570
from the mediastinum.

00:12:50.590 --> 00:12:53.340
This is done by sharp dissection.

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:55.420
There is some bleeding, we catch the,

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:58.430
the small vessels as we encounter them.

00:12:58.450 --> 00:13:02.140
We free this up to, to the inferior pulmonary vein,

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:05.800
which is the most inferior and posterior of the structures.

00:13:05.820 --> 00:13:08.980
Now we're coming up to the inferior pulmonary vein.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:10.850
You can see it here exposed.

00:13:10.870 --> 00:13:14.350
There is a node distally which seems soft

00:13:14.370 --> 00:13:16.650
and I don't believe is involved.

00:13:16.670 --> 00:13:19.870
A ligature is placed around the inferior pulmonary vein.

00:13:19.890 --> 00:13:23.030
It is held laterally.

00:13:23.050 --> 00:13:26.790
Another one is placed medially.

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:46.070
Both these are tied, one laterally and one medially.

00:13:46.090 --> 00:13:48.950
And then, as in the pulmonary artery,

00:13:48.970 --> 00:13:55.350
we will put two transfixion sutures medially on the cardiac side.

00:13:55.370 --> 00:13:59.480
This one goes superiorly inferiorly.

00:13:59.500 --> 00:14:02.100
It's tied anteriorly and then will be pulled around

00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:10.710
and tied posteriorly.

00:14:10.730 --> 00:14:22.270
This is pulling the suture around and tying it posteriorly.

00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:25.500
One should give sufficient time the ligature to seat,

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:41.710
because this is a big vessel and it must be pulled down tautly.

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:43.710
Now this is another suture.

00:14:43.730 --> 00:14:47.540
This one goes posteriorly anteriorly so that the four quadrants of

00:14:47.560 --> 00:15:09.330
the vessel are caught.

00:15:09.350 --> 00:15:13.140
A munion clamp has been applied to the vessel distally.

00:15:13.160 --> 00:15:15.230
The ligatures are divided,

00:15:15.250 --> 00:15:19.730
and then the inferior pulmonary vein is divided immediately adjacent

00:15:19.750 --> 00:15:21.310
to the munion clamp,

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:23.660
leaving a relatively long segment

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:27.780
on the cardiac side.

00:15:27.800 --> 00:15:28.530
Now it's divided,

00:15:28.550 --> 00:15:32.830
and one can see the sutured end of the inferior pulmonary vein.

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:37.990
We next go up and isolate the superior pulmonary vein

00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:40.750
and do the same thing.

00:15:40.770 --> 00:15:44.210
We put two ligatures on, one medially, one distally,

00:15:44.230 --> 00:15:48.620
two transfixion sutures medially, a munion clamp distally,

00:15:48.640 --> 00:15:52.140
and then division of the superior pulmonary vein.

00:15:52.160 --> 00:15:56.150
Same technique is used for all three vessels.

00:15:56.170 --> 00:15:59.070
Now the superior pulmonary vein has been divided,

00:15:59.090 --> 00:16:03.000
the vascular structures are now separated from the lung,

00:16:03.020 --> 00:16:06.230
with exception of the bronchial arteries.

00:16:06.250 --> 00:16:10.920
We now go back and isolate and free the bronchus.

00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:15.270
The left mainstem bronchus up to the region of the bifurcation,

00:16:15.290 --> 00:16:19.880
because we don't want to leave a long stump.

00:16:19.900 --> 00:16:22.660
We are now freeing the bronchus from areolar tissue

00:16:22.680 --> 00:16:27.280
and any nodes that might be in the hilar area.

00:16:27.300 --> 00:16:28.710
Apparently there are none.

00:16:28.730 --> 00:16:32.090
A bronchial clamp is placed across the bronchus,

00:16:32.110 --> 00:16:34.580
relatively high behind the carina.

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:37.750
A second one is placed distal to it,

00:16:37.770 --> 00:16:42.140
because we don't want any spillage from the lung.

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:59.200
And then the bronchus is divided by means of this right-angled knife.

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:04.010
Now we have to close the bronchus.

00:17:04.030 --> 00:17:07.030
Whereas previously we used mattress sutures,

00:17:07.050 --> 00:17:12.710
now we use plastic sutures and this is a, these are multiple sutures.

00:17:12.730 --> 00:17:17.570
The first one is placed at one end of the clamp and tied.

00:17:17.590 --> 00:17:21.630
Now because this is plastic, one must use many throws.

00:17:21.650 --> 00:17:24.260
It's one of the disadvantages of the plastic suture

00:17:24.280 --> 00:17:27.280
but the main advantage is that it is well-tolerated,

00:17:27.300 --> 00:17:29.620
and since we've been using this technique

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:35.990
we've had no trouble with the bronchial stumps.

00:17:36.010 --> 00:17:39.960
It is important that these sutures be brought up tautly enough

00:17:39.980 --> 00:17:42.020
just to approximate the,

00:17:42.040 --> 00:17:45.680
the edges of the bronchus but not too tightly to cut through.

00:17:45.700 --> 00:17:55.290
One can cut through the bronchus if one pulls too tightly.

00:17:55.310 --> 00:17:58.200
Now a suture is placed at the other end of the bronchus,

00:17:58.220 --> 00:18:02.130
which is tied around the end of the bronchus,

00:18:02.150 --> 00:18:04.930
in the same way the first suture was tied.

00:18:04.950 --> 00:18:09.130
Then we put in a row of sutures proximal to the clamp,

00:18:09.150 --> 00:18:13.140
but these are not tied until after the clamp is removed.

00:18:13.160 --> 00:18:16.460
They are tagged by means of small hemostats,

00:18:16.480 --> 00:18:23.870
so that we won't get them mixed up.

00:18:23.890 --> 00:18:27.880
As I said previously, at one time we used mattress sutures,

00:18:27.900 --> 00:18:31.750
but we don't anymore because we feel that interfered with the blood supply.

00:18:31.770 --> 00:18:33.990
Now the clamp is removed and the sutures

00:18:34.010 --> 00:18:36.420
which been placed are held taut

00:18:36.440 --> 00:18:38.040
and in this way there is

00:18:38.060 --> 00:18:47.570
little danger of leakage from the bronchus while we ligate the sutures.

00:18:47.590 --> 00:18:49.980
Again I want to emphasize that these are tied

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:53.970
only tight enough to approximate the walls of the bronchus

00:18:53.990 --> 00:19:00.040
without cutting through them.

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:08.540
They are all tied individually.

00:19:08.560 --> 00:19:10.620
The fact that they have been tagged with hemostats before

00:19:10.640 --> 00:19:13.050
makes it easy for us to identify them

00:19:13.070 --> 00:19:15.530
and not get them mixed up with one another.

00:19:15.550 --> 00:19:18.760
This is of importance, I think.

00:19:18.780 --> 00:19:23.230
Now they've all been tied and one can see that the closure is very good.

00:19:23.250 --> 00:19:25.720
Now we're putting in saline

00:19:25.740 --> 00:19:28.380
because we want to be sure that this is watertight.

00:19:28.400 --> 00:19:33.230
The anesthetist is now asked to increase the intrabronchial pressure

00:19:33.250 --> 00:19:36.010
by increasing the pressure on the bag

00:19:36.030 --> 00:19:37.370
and you can see there is no leak.

00:19:37.390 --> 00:19:40.690
This is absolutely necessary because if there is any leak,

00:19:40.710 --> 00:19:45.310
we must put additional sutures in to close it.

00:19:45.330 --> 00:19:52.540
Since there is no leak we can now remove the sutures,

00:19:52.560 --> 00:20:01.840
leaving them relatively long since they are plastic.

00:20:01.860 --> 00:20:05.370
And now we close over the bronchial stump

00:20:05.390 --> 00:20:08.740
by suturing the flaps of pleura

00:20:08.760 --> 00:20:11.380
both anteriorly and posteriorly over it.

00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:13.610
This I think is extremely important

00:20:13.630 --> 00:20:19.550
because it tends to protect the bronchial stump and prevent a blowout.

00:20:19.570 --> 00:20:22.070
I think as a result of this, uh,

00:20:22.090 --> 00:20:26.190
serous fluid is kept trapped in this area,

00:20:26.210 --> 00:20:30.460
which becomes deposited as fibrin

00:20:30.480 --> 00:20:32.380
and then ultimately organized

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:37.830
so that it's actually like a cap over the bronchial stump.

00:20:37.850 --> 00:20:39.860
We're now ready to close the chest.

00:20:39.880 --> 00:20:42.870
Since we've done a pneumonectomy we do not drain the chest,

00:20:42.890 --> 00:20:47.250
but we put a tube in anteriorly until the chest has been closed.

00:20:47.270 --> 00:20:49.220
And the reason for this is that

00:20:49.240 --> 00:20:52.250
with the patient in the right lateral decubitus,

00:20:52.270 --> 00:20:56.360
the mediastinum gravitates toward the right side,

00:20:56.380 --> 00:20:59.200
and if this were allowed to continue,

00:20:59.220 --> 00:21:03.480
there would be accumulation of air in the left hemothorax.

00:21:03.500 --> 00:21:04.940
The dressing is applied,

00:21:04.960 --> 00:21:08.850
the patient is then placed back in the supine position,

00:21:08.870 --> 00:21:13.160
the anesthetist is then asked to inflate the opposite lung,

00:21:13.180 --> 00:21:17.210
which brings the mediastinum back to the midline,

00:21:17.230 --> 00:21:22.010
and then this tube which we put in temporarily is removed.

00:21:22.030 --> 00:21:24.590
This brings the mediastinum back in the position

00:21:24.610 --> 00:21:30.660
that it should be, and the chest is now sealed.

00:21:30.680 --> 00:21:33.790
We want the cavity to fill up with serous fluid.

00:21:33.810 --> 00:21:35.150
Fibrin will become deposited

00:21:35.170 --> 00:21:38.530
and then ultimately there will be organization of this area.

00:21:38.550 --> 00:21:40.450
Of course, if this were a lobectomy

00:21:40.470 --> 00:21:44.140
we would use tubes to evacuate the cavity.

00:21:44.160 --> 00:21:47.480
Now here is the lung, you can see it's a large tumor.

00:21:47.500 --> 00:21:51.350
I'm cutting through the attachment of the pleura

00:21:51.370 --> 00:21:54.710
and we're coming down to the mass,

00:21:54.730 --> 00:21:58.630
and now we can see why this tumor increased in size.

00:21:58.650 --> 00:22:01.750
On our right is a, one can see the neoplasm.

00:22:01.770 --> 00:22:04.170
On the left is a hematoma.

00:22:04.190 --> 00:22:08.420
And this increase in size that was noticed within a relatively short period of time

00:22:08.440 --> 00:22:11.200
is due to the hemorrhage within the neoplasm.

00:22:11.220 --> 00:22:19.960
It'd be impossible for our tumor to grow that fast.

00:22:19.980 --> 00:22:22.630
I'd like to again emphasize that the results

00:22:22.650 --> 00:22:26.640
from the treatment of carcinoma of the lung are not good.

00:22:26.660 --> 00:22:29.170
As I said, only six percent of them are alive and well

00:22:29.190 --> 00:22:30.660
at the end of five years.

00:22:30.680 --> 00:22:36.570
It is for this reason that it behooves all of us to use the one weapon we have,

00:22:36.590 --> 00:22:39.210
namely the prevention of cancer of the lung.

00:22:39.230 --> 00:22:44.150
Which is preventable almost entirely by absence of smoking.

00:22:44.170 --> 00:22:48.490
Because as I mentioned before with the exception of adenocarcinoma.

00:22:48.510 --> 00:22:56.510
the the uh, all cancers of the lung are due to cigarette smoking.

